{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Methane Bubble Curtain: Seep Microbes of the Cascadia Margin",
    "date": "2026-10-30",
    "lesson": "Off Oregon's Hydrate Ridge, methane leaks from the seafloor in bubble streams, feeding a narrow band of chemosynthetic bacteria that oxidize methane and sulfide instead of eating organic food. These chemolithotrophs cluster exactly where the upward plume of dissolved gas overlaps a thin, deep sliver of residual light penetrating the 600m water column; everywhere else they starve. Meanwhile free-living heterotrophs scavenge the carcasses of methane-seep clams and tubeworm debris that drift down. This level pairs a chemosynthetic founder that must hold position in a vertical plume against slow currents with carbohydrate/protein scavengers working the fall debris above the vent floor, showing how chemosynthesis and ordinary heterotrophy coexist in the same water column, feeding off completely different energy sources.",
    "citation": "Levin et al. 2016, Cascadia margin methane seep ecology; Hydrate Ridge seep microbiology studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Hydrate Ridge methane seep, Cascadia Margin, offshore Oregon, ~780m depth"
  },
